  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>Precious One was two weeks old at the point of writing.</p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-n">&lt;1,000</div><div class="stat-l">Spotify streams</div></div>
    <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-n">32</div><div class="stat-l">Monthly listeners</div></div>
    <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-n">6%</div><div class="stat-l">Popularity score</div></div>
    <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-n">#1</div><div class="stat-l">Most-streamed track</div></div>
  </div>

  <p>None of the 15 pitches received a response. For a release with no existing press profile, no prior coverage to reference, and a two-week campaign window, that is about what to expect. The contacts have the track. The second pitch, on the next release, will have context behind it.</p>

  <p>The response that did come was off-platform. Omjine received direct messages and comments from listeners saying the song meant something to them. At this scale, people going out of their way to say that is worth paying attention to.</p>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <h2>What Ardito Learned</h2>

  <div class="learned-item">
    <strong>Press shot first.</strong>
    <p>It came up mid-campaign with no quick fix available. Several radio contacts require one and outreach to them was limited as a result. This is now confirmed at onboarding before anything else moves.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="learned-item">
    <strong>PRO registration is almost always missing.</strong>
    <p>Most independent artists at this stage have not registered with their national collecting society. Without it, every radio play goes uncompensated. For Omjine it was KOMCA. For a UK-based artist it would be PRS and PPL. It takes one conversation to fix and needs to happen before the campaign starts.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="learned-item">
    <strong>Quality over quantity on pitching.</strong>
    <p>15 researched, specific emails is more useful than 50 generic ones. Publications can tell the difference between someone who read their work and someone who found their email address.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="learned-item">
    <strong>No replies does not mean no impact.</strong>
    <p>The value of the first campaign is building the contact list, getting the track in front of the right people, and having something to reference on the second release. The second pitch to the same contacts will land differently.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="learned-item">
    <strong>Blog and radio need different assets.</strong>
    <p>Blogs get a private SoundCloud link. Radio gets an MP3 attached directly, plus a Google Drive link, artwork, press shot, and bio. Getting this wrong wastes the contact.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <h2>What Comes Next</h2>
  <p>Sync submissions are planned for Music Vine, Musicbed, Artlist, and Songtradr using the instrumental version. After that, direct outreach to UK sync agencies including Music Gateway and A&G Sync.</p>
  <p>Streaming data will be reviewed at four weeks post-release to shape the approach for her next campaign.</p>

